  Item Metadata
  ADM-1 Open-ended Age 80+
  Group
  General Notes This COD-PS dataset includes an additional table at the metropolis level, which
  contains projected population counts for two major cities in Cameroon, Ville de
  Douala and Ville de Yaoundé.
  Data Limitations Cameroon has not conducted a census since 2005, and preparations for the next
  census are ongoing. As limited information is available on demographic dynamics
  since the last census, and the baseline population was measured 20 years ago,
  assumptions around post-censal demographic change are subject to notable
  uncertainty. For example, while there has been substantial internal migration in
  Cameroon since 2005, the subnational projections in this COD-PS assume zero net
  internal migration. This may impact the projected ADM-1 population counts by age
  and sex included in this COD-PS.
  COD-PS Demographic Data The COD-PS was assessed against ADM-0 estimates and projections published in
  Quality Assessment the World Population Prospects (2024 Revision) by the United Nations Department
  of Economic and Social Affairs (UN-DESA), and found to be largely consistent at the
  ADM-0 and ADM-1 level.
  2. Methodological Documentation
  Item Methodological Documentation
  Methodology Used The cohort component method was used to construct the projections upon which
  this COD-PS is based. These projections are based on the 2005 census, and were
  updated for the period 2016-2025 based on survey data (Demographic and Health
  Surveys and Multiple Cluster Indicator Surveys) up to 2014. Region (ADM-1)
  projections were calibrated to align with national projections.
  Baseline Population 2005 census
  Post-enumeration survey A PES on a representative 2% sample of the population was carried out from
  (PES) November 2006 - January 2007. Detailed results from the PES were not found
  online.
  Assessment and Adjustment The United Nations combined age-sex accuracy index was 29.81 for the 2005
  of the Baseline Population census, indicating somewhat inaccurate age reporting. No recorded adjustments to
  the baseline population were found online.
  Fertility (births) Between 1991 and 2014, the total fertility rate (TFR) in Cameroon remained relatively
  high, though it declined from 5.9 children per woman in 1991 to 5.1 children per
  woman in 2011 and 4.9 children per woman in 2014. The medium scenario for these
  projections assumes a slight decline in TFR nationally, from 4.9 in 2014 to 4.4 in
  2025, with most regions also projected to experience an overall decline in TFR 2014-
  2025 except for the Nord and Extrême-Nord Regions.
