  --- page 3 ---
  Item Methodological Documentation
  Mortality (deaths) The NSO expects life expectancy at birth to rise, from 53.4 years (male) and 57.1
  years (female) in 2005 to 57.1 years (male) and 60.6 years (female) in 2025. It was
  assumed that the HIV prevalence observed in 2011 will fall by half in 2025 both at the
  national level and in each region.
  International migration (net The NSO assumed net international migration to be zero, due to the lack of sufficient
  migration) data.
  Internal migration (migration The NSO assumed net internal migration to be zero, due to the lack of sufficient data
  within country) on internal migration dynamics within Cameroon.
  3. Intrinsic Population Growth Rates (ADM-0 & 1)
  At ADM-0, the implied post-censal (2005-2025) population growth rates (2.61% per year: female
  2.64%, male 2.58%) are generally consistent with the rates implied by the UNʼs population projections
  (2006: 2.75%, 2012: 2.82%, 2018: 2.81%, 2025: 2.54%) published in the 2024 revision of the World
  Population Prospects (WPP).
  At ADM-1, during the post-censal period the NSO projects the highest growth rates for the regions of
  Nord (3.62% per year: female 3.68%, male 3.57%), Centre (3.05% per year: female 3.08%, male
  3.02%), and Extrême-Nord (2.84% per year: female 2.81%, male 2.88%). The regions of Nord and
  Extrême-Nord are projected to have the highest fertility among regions over the projection period, likely
  contributing to this relatively high expected growth (projected TFR for 2025 is 6.7 in Extrême-Nord and
  6.0 in Nord). High growth for the Centre Region overall is likely driven in part by expectations of
  continued high growth of the capital city Yaoundé.
  The lowest growth rates, conversely, are projected for the regions of Ouest (1.29% per year: female
  1.39%, male 1.20%), followed by Sud (1.67% per year: female 1.60%, male 1.73%) and Nord-Ouest
  (1.69% per year: female 1.80%, male 1.58%).
